for 12 years this statue of Robert  baden-powell has perched here on pool  key without complaint overlooking Brown  Sea Island where he founded the scouting  movement calls for it to be removed  because of his alleged racism homophobia  and support of Adolf Hitler were given  short shrift by locals we feel very  strongly about this statue it's here and  is in that position for a reason it  faces Brown Sea Island which is where  the Scout Movement was founded by baden  powell his history has nothing to do  with the removal of this stature he  wanted to stay there's nobody against it  they want it here some scouts felt so  strongly they traveled from South Wales  to voice their support as soon as I saw  it on the Facebook last night with the  newspaper article I knew I had to come  down and voice my opinion it's  disgusting it really is more than 10,000  people have now signed an online  petition urging the council not to move  the statue well this is the nearest pub  to the baden-powell statue the Lord  Nelson you don't have to travel far in  England to find signs of the country's  past and in Norfolk there are calls now  for two statues of Nelson to be taken  down  in Bristol the council has now fished  the toppled statue of  seventeenth-century slave owner  Edward Colston out of the water so it  can be placed in a museum and in Cardiff  the Lord Mayor is leading calls for a  statue of slave owner Thomas picton to  be removed from Cardiff City Hall Hey  inside City Hall we haven't called the  Hall of Heroes where there are statues  of eminent figures from Wales is history  and almost in we think Thomas picton  doesn't belong amongst them I don't  think he's a hero  I think his actions were were evil  opponent I think's more appropriate he  would have moved from from the marble  Hall where he is now and taken to a  museum it's more appropriate the context  of what he's done can be more clearly  understood  Sky News contacted 43 councils with a  total of 58 statues between them which  have been criticized for their links to  racism slavery or colonialism of the 33  councils that responded 28 said they  were now undertaking a review of at  least some of the statues in their area  or were planning to consult local  residents about their future five  councils said they were not planning to  review the locations or appropriateness  of the statues in their area  the council here in Poole says it only  wants to move the statue because it  fears it could be targeted and toppled  but locals fear that if it's taken away  it won't return and neither will the  thousands of tourists who come here  every year to celebrate the founding  father of the scouting movement and a  Brady Sky news Indore
 

